<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utah Territory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Territory of Utah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> Territory was an organised territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> created by the Compromise of 1850 from part of the lands taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, replacing the Mormon-proposed State of Deseret. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> was its first governor until federal troops arrived in the Utah War of 1857–1858. Originally stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> to the Rockies, it was reduced as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and Wyoming were formed, and it became the state of Utah in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utah-territory.jpg" alt="Utah Territory" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mangoman88; Theshibboleth; Tsujigiri · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Utah_Territory,_vector_image_-_2011.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> was an organised incorporated territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 9 September 1850 until 4 January 1896, when it was admitted as the 45th state. At its creation it included all of present-day Utah, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a> apart from a part of the south including the Las Vegas area, much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and the extreme southwest corner of Wyoming. The area had been inhabited by indigenous people for over 12,000 years, including the Puebloan builders of stone masonry and the Fremont culture, who left after a long drought in the 1200s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a>, Paiute and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> settled later, and Juan Antonio de Rivera of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> is the first known European to enter, in 1765.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mormon pioneers began settling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a> in 1847 and relied on institutions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a> in place of secular civil government. They drafted a constitution for a State of Deseret in 1849, with proposed borders covering the Great Basin and the Colorado River watershed, and petitioned Congress under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> to admit it. Congress instead organised the territory as part of the Compromise of 1850, which sought to preserve the balance between slave and free states, without mentioning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. President Millard Fillmore signed the act on the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> was admitted.</p><p>Young was inaugurated as first governor. The capital was at Fillmore from 1850 to 1856, then moved to Salt Lake City. Mormon governance was controversial in the rest of the country, partly because of newspaper depictions of the polygamous marriages practised by the settlers.</p><p>Non-Mormon settlers were drawn to the west by the 1858 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> discovery at the Comstock Lode, and the Nevada Territory was created from the western part in 1861 as the federal government sought more control over silver mining. Gold at Breckenridge in 1859 brought miners from the east, and in 1861 Congress transferred a large part of the eastern territory to the new Colorado Territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1869 the territorial legislature approved women’s suffrage, and on 12 February 1870 the right to vote was extended to free white women who were US citizens aged 21 or older. Seraph Young Ford, a schoolteacher, cast her ballot in the Salt Lake City municipal election on 14 February 1870, the first woman to vote under an equal suffrage law in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> stayed a territory 46 years, much longer than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a>, admitted in 1864, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, admitted in 1876. The beehive, chosen as the emblem of the provisional State of Deseret in 1848, stands for industry, and the sego lilies beside it for peace; both appear on the state coat of arms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Utah Territory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Smith was killed by a mob in 1844, the largest group followed Brigham Young and emigrated to what became the <b>Utah Territory</b>, where the church began the open practice of plural marriage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mormonism">Mormonism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Wyoming Territory gave women the vote in 1869 and <b>Utah Territory</b> in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Congress rejected statehood and in the Compromise of 1850 created the much smaller <b>Utah Territory</b>, with Young as governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-deseret">State of Deseret</a></li></ul></section>
